# Secrets defined here are available for reference under registry/password, env/secret, builder/secrets,
# and accessories/*/env/secret in config/deploy.yml. All secrets should be pulled from either
# password manager, ENV, or a file. DO NOT ENTER RAW CREDENTIALS HERE! This file needs to be safe for git.
#
# Since the app stopped carrying encrypted credentials there is nothing left
# for this file to read off disk, so it holds no assignments at all. What it is
# now is the list of names a deployment has to supply, and where:
#
#   KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD        an access token for the image registry
#   SECRET_KEY_BASE                signs cookies and password-reset tokens
#   RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD   authenticates the Postmark inbound webhook
#   POSTMARK_SMTP_TOKEN            sends password-reset mail
#   SIFTBOX_READER_EMAIL           the reader's account, seeded at first boot
#   SIFTBOX_READER_PASSWORD
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY              writes the morning edition
#   ELEVENLABS_API_KEY             reads an edition aloud when asked
#   HONEYBADGER_API_KEY            reports errors
#
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID is not here: it is a public identifier rather than a
# secret, so it lives in env.clear with the SIFTBOX_* names and is overridden
# in the destination file.
#
# The raw values go in .kamal/secrets-common, which is gitignored and which
# every deploy reads whether or not it names a destination.
#
# Which file Kamal reads after it depends on the destination, and this one is
# only ever the destination-less case:
#
#   bin/kamal deploy                  .kamal/secrets-common, then this file
#   bin/kamal deploy -d production    .kamal/secrets-common, then
#                                     .kamal/secrets.production
#
# So a value written here is simply ignored by a deploy with -d, and nothing
# says so: the file is never opened. Keep the values in secrets-common, which
# both paths read, and this stays a difference that cannot bite.
#
# Either way the second file is merged over the first, so a name given here
# would take precedence over the real value — which is why every name above is
# a comment. Restating one as a $VAR passthrough resolves against an unset
# shell variable and overwrites the real value with an empty string.
#
# Kamal refuses to deploy when a name in env.secret is in none of the files it
# read, so a name left out of secrets-common fails loudly. A wrong value does
# not: see docs/deploying.md step 4.

